2013.07.09
	* STEAC, GLASS, MAC, SD: following JComputBiol.2012.632.pdf, we implemented these distance-bsaed methods in the
	program main_maxtree (which previously worked with alignments, and employed only MaxTree (GLASS)). The four methods
	differ in how they resolve multiple divergence times for a species pair within the locus and between loci. They can be
	resolved by chosing the minimum value, or the average value between the alternatives. Each method suggests a
	clustering -- single-linkage for those that use the minimum among loci and upgma for those that use the avberage. 
	* patristic distances inside a tree: the distances between all leaf pairs can be calculated very efficiently by using
	the vector of leaves as they appear in postorder (the Euler tour with leaves only): each internal node can then point
	to the two vector indices corresp. to the first and last leaves below it. The patristic distances can then be
	calculated by storing the distances from all nodes to the root node, and using this fast listing of leaves below a
	node. My version of the algorithm works also for trees without branch lengths (we can use the internodal distances).
2013.07.08
	* scaled branch lengths: treespace now stores branch lengths scaled by the total tree length (sum of branches). 
	The unscaled tree lengths are stored on treespace->tlen[] vector that has 3 consecutive values: the mean, min and max.
	Notice that this is distinct from branches, where the mean, min and max are not consecutive but the whole vectors
	'concatenated'.
	* single-linkage clustering: now the upgma_from_dist() function has a boolean that when TRUE uses the nearest-neighbor
	or single-linkage or minimum distance, instead of the mean distance (UPGMA) between clusters. This is to emulate STEAC
	and friends.
