2013.06.06
	* argtable: Now the program can work with command-line arguments, and do not need a control file. If both are present,
	command-line options have precedence. 
	* distance model: Now the user can decide which distances she wants to consider, by giving a vector of zeroes and
	ones, like 1110001, which are, in order: (ndups, nloss, ndeepcoals, RF, hdist, hdist_initialcost, SPR) where the SPR
	is our approx SPR distance (doesn't work well for gene trees much larger than sptrees), and hdist are the edge
	matching costs we use as we calculate the dSPR. The default is 1111000 and if the user changes it she MUST remember
	it when asking for the program to analyse the checkpoint file. (This gave me an idea that a True© mixture model would
	consider all these combinatorial assignments)
	* mixture deactivated: even with label shuffling (described in 2013.06.02 potential bug) the behaviour was not good
	since I was sampling new lambda values even when the associated w tended to zero -- that is, anything goes. The w's
	themselves are known to behave badly and once reach zero are hard to go back (discusesd in Xian book and I think in
	10.1.1.147.687.pdf which is an expanded version of this book chapter). We could try to sample them directly from
	gammas but still I don't know how to sample the lambdas when they are in practice "off" by the w's (all books work
	with the augmented distrib where indicator z tells which mixture element is the active one... maybe I could update in
	several steps, given each one of the elements, but that's overkill specially considering the exchange algo). So now I
	just use the multivariate exponential model (e^{d1/l1 + d2/l2 + ...}). Now continuous variables are bound to be larger
	than INVALID_SMALL (a #define currently at 1.e-9) to avoid zero -- if proposed value is smaller, I simply reject it.
	* comparison between models: by doing a few simulations using just annealing, the multivariate exponential model gave
	me much better answer than the mixture -- here I had to set the concentration parameter at 10 and not updating it
	-- since it will go small values and the W's will become zero except for one. The answer was very similar to iGTP,
	with iGTP being correct a few more times but in some cases when both failed  my model could find a (wrong but) closer tree.
	I need to redo this analysis since I was not scaling the distances (diving by nleaves).
2013.06.02
	* Simulated annealing: given final 1/kT = F, initial 1/kT = I, and number of iterations = N, the temperature is given
	by "I + (F-I) * log(n)/log(N)" at iteration n>0. It's possible to run only simulated annealing (by letting n_iter=0)
	and furthermore have several "samples" (that is cycles starting at n=1 to n=N) that will be saved to checkpoint file
	when n_iter=0. (if n_iter>0 then we don't save it to avoid messing with the posterior distrib). Implemented only for
	IS, which now also have burnin period (previous versions before 2013.06 neglected burnin). It's possible but dummy to
	have initial 1/kT higher than final -- if one wants dispersed initial values for the real Bayesian, since SA messes with 
	that, then one might let both final and initial 1/kT be lower than 1 (let's say I=0.001 and F=0.1, with e.g. N=1). At
	each cycle (defined by ctrl->sa_n_samples) the perturbation parameter of continuous variables is increased according
	to "Pn = P0 X alpha^n" where n is the cycle -- so we fix the final parameter P_N to be 10 times the initial one.
	The user can set n_iter=0 to run only the simulated annealing -- many times according to sa_n_samples, each time 
	saving the final result.
	* dynamic n_mc_samples: the chain length for the secondary MCMC, responsible for the auxiliary gene tree sampling, is
	now variable in the main sampler (between 1 and the value chosen by the user). On the burnin this number is half the
	defined by user, and in the annealing this number is zero -- that is, the partition function is not taken into account.
	The user can also define n_mc_samples=0, in which case the whole program neglects the partition function. 
	* truncated exponential: specially when n_mc_samples=0, we might want to have our unnormalized distrib as close as possible 
	to a normalized one. Looking at my old notes I now include the truncation term for the exponential distrib, which is
	equal to [1-e(-MAX/lambda)] on the denominator. In our case we don't know MAX but we assume it's close to one (since
	we divide n_dli[] by dli_max[]). I haven't noticed any change (actually I included it since the lambdas were exploding
	due to a bug when n_mc_samples=0, that I discovered and fixed only afterwards...) <-- REMOVED on 2013.06.04 since
	don't seem to change anything, the problem with large negative values for log(prob) was related to very small lambdas
	and weights (which is now fixed by rejecting tiny values, and removal of troublesome mixture distribution).
	* potential BUG: the weights are not moving well -- in example 20130423.example_to_test_spr/00.easy, once one of them
	is one and the others are ~zero, they don't move. Need to create other proposals (from gammas?) since we expect them
	to change even for a very low concentration parameter. And the acceptance ratio is high, which means that they are
	accepting silly moves that do not explore the space. FIXED 2013.06.03: it was the same bug when n_mc_samples=0; I
	included an index shuffle nonetheless to allow for weights being exchanged between mixture elements.
