2013.05.18
	* MPI: paralell version of the guenomu program now available with autoconf option "--enable-mpi". The parallelization
	works by each job using a subset of gene families, and communicating only when updating common parameters like species
	tree and reconciliation parameters. It's an improvement over the serial version when there are many gene families or a
	few large ones. In any case, each job will receive the same number of gene families, and there should be more families
	than jobs. When analysing the output, the parallel program should be invoked with the same number of jobs -- such that
	each job can analyze a checkpoint file for the same gene families. The output files wil lhave some redundant
	information like species trees. The ideal would be to partition the gene families s.t. each job would spend almost the
	same time per iteration, even if the number of gene families per jobs varied wildly, but it seems that this problem is
	not so easy to solve. We order the gene families by size, so we at least send the largest ones to different jobs.
	* mixing of a chain: The problem I reported previously about the program not moving might be a "feature" of the model:
	In the data set I studied I had the same family tree "repeated" which makes the posterior distribution very sharp.
	With treefam data sets I didn't observe this problem, and guenomu finds the same trees as iGTP -- besides showing
	other possible species trees. GMTM still "stops moving" after it finds a good tree, even if it other proposals do
	change the species tree...
2013.05.07
	* SPR move: there was a bug with the unrooted SPR for the 4 taxa case, to which I added an exception. I discovered
	when analyzing treefam data base. BTW the program is very slow and stalls after a local optimum is found. (global?
	must compare to iGTP at least).
	* GMTM sptree move: we can apply an "general" MTM for species trees since we don't need to sample auxGenetree for
	every trial -- they'll cancel out and we just need to sample from the proposed sptree and compare it to the current
	sptree. Furthermore if we have our weight function proportional to the unscaled P(G/S) we can simplify a bit more.
	Remember that the weight doesn't need to be a prob distribution, can be any measure -- provided it will help and not
	disturb the choice of good values...
	* BUG: the program stops moving (species trees are reject), as described above, and for GMTM it happens much sooner. I
	also found (and fixed) a bug on the concentration parameter update: it was not taking into account wb[] -- that is, it
	was data-free... maybe because it's a term like (wa'-1) X sum_i( log(wb[i]) ) -- and I concluded that the sum was
	equal to one...
	* Nmixt: Now we have two global variables (#define actually): Ndist for the distances and Nmixt for the mixture terms.
	That's because the mixture term duploss depends on two distances, dups and losses (it makes no sense separate those).
	Each distance still has its lambda, though. 
2013.04.29
	* Dirichlet mixture: implemented a "scaled" version where weights are constrained to sum up to one -- but constrain is
	not on model (at first they could add to something other than one, they are just one term in log, and in the end the
	exchange algo cancel them out), but on proposal of new state. This way a term like the Det of the Jacobian appears
	-- sum of betas elevated to the dimension -- but refer to proposal, and are not heated. 
	* sptree move: in a particular simulation, the chain was stuck in a (global?) optimal sptree, therefore I devised a
	more audatious branch swap -- the same as the one used in addTreeNoise.c, but now every branch has the same
	probability of change. The algo traverses the tree while tries to swaps all branches acc to this probability, that
	increases in case no swap is done, until successful. --> On this data set this new function didn't work, now I'm
	checking to see if it's a bug, bad model or what.
	* other changes: now the number of dimensions is given by Ndist, and by changing it we can easily include/exclude more
	distances -- exception to stdout functions? Concentration parameters (one per gene, controling Dirichlet) are also
	updated, I don't think that updating their prior is also necessary (it would be just an Exp[], but allowing
	concentration parameters too low might not be wise -- LogNormal[] maybe?) Although P(G/S) is unscaled and needs an
	exchange algo, I decided to include the 1/lambda (from exp(x/lambda/lambda of the exponential distrib) just to keep
	the prob values within bounds (not too far away from one) and therefore do not lose precision. No function from
	mcmc_proposal.c should have access to the distance calculations or original distance parameters -- they should receive
	them from store_gene_sptree_distances() of mcmc_common.c . That is, what the distances mean should be opaque to the
	proposal functions. And the probability should come only from prob_term_prior_dist_from_vectors() or prob_term_prior_dist().
