2013.04.21
	* bugs fixed: previous versions were not calculating properly the exchange algorithm in update_lambda() -- the lines
	were commented out, and when included led to a segfault after long runs. The culprit was a malformed tree (internal
	node without children), which pointed to a problem with the topol_int[] and n_dli[][] vectors, consecutive in memory
	and therefore might be overwritting one another. OTOH I checked with very longer vector sizes and the problem
	remained -- this may haunt us in the future. The solution was to never use the exchange algo as log(probA) - log (probB), 
	but as log (probA/probB) since some terms can be cancelled out -- e.g. the distances should be treated as diffs
	whenever possible...
	We should also be careful with the tree->index vector, since it is used by spr_unrooted() and
	therefore cannot be used to store topology information. I also found a bug in the mtm_ratio(), since it might due to
	roudoff errors return an index larger than the mtm[] matrix.
	* todo: 1) update the counters (global vars), since they are very outdated, from the first non-IS algorithm. 2) Include SPR
	in the algorithm. 3) Create global (to the mcmc_proposal.c file) vectors to avoid malloc overhead (but a gprof showed
	that the slowest part is the has_mix() function, together with other update_topol() and then the DLI distance
	calculation.) 4) avoid whenever possible calculating the whole probability, preferring the probability ratios -- since
	many terms can cancel out and we focus just on terms that will differ. 
	* DONE (04.22): (1) and (2) from above. (3) might not be needed (not now, at least). (4) is constant vigilance. 
2013.04.17
	* hungarian method: implemented, and used to match edges from one tree to another. Haven't tested with gene/species
	trees, only with same leafset trees. I tried several scalings over this optimal cost, no one very good. At my machine
	at home I have the impression that the values (optimal match costs) are lower than at the lab...
	The optimal matching can also restrict the disagreement comparisons -- instead of
	N^2 disagreement splits for all possible combinations we have only N, which corresponds to the optimal matching. The
	calculation becomes much faster (let's say 4 times faster for 63 leaves), and the performance is almost the same
	-- maybe a little higher variance around the true value. (due to a bg in the hungarian algo, I concluded at some point
	that the matching might not be so good. I was wrong, and the bug is solved.)
	* SPR improvement: in function split_find_small_agreement(), we use now bipartition_contains_bits() to check if prune
	bipartition belongs to same side of tree or to distinct sides. That is, we check if it corresponds to one SPR or two,
	and increase SPR counter accordingly. It seems to improve calculation in any circumstance, specially for large values
	of applied SPR -- at the cost of some	overestimation which didn't happen before. A good compromise is to have these
	extra SPRs counted in a separate variable (split->spr_extra) and then the function calling the SPR distance can handle
	-- use split->spr or split->spr+split->spr_extra or even split->spr+split->spr_extra/2 . 
