2012.09.22 Leo Martins
	* DONE from 2012.09.20: working with ILS (under the MC integration algorithm): now all functions -- including exp
	penalty function -- use dups, losses, and deepcoals (=ils). We must be careful with interpretation: large values for
	lambda_prior for a given distance  mean that we must relax the penalty to work, that is, that probably the model
	cannot be adequately explained by this distance: the underlying factor might be the other distances with higher
	penalties (= lower lambdas). This is confusing since we are tempted to understand the lambdas as "weights" (the higher
	it is, the more important; while the opposite is true). And we should also look for the _lower_ values of distances of
	likely explanations: for instance gene trees simulated under ILS will show dli vector = [5,0,0] which means that it
	can be explained by 5 dups and 0 losses OR by 0 deepcoals (when reading that, we can discard duplosses <-- neglecting
	composite cases). 
2012.09.20 Leo Martins
	* fix: the "bug" commented below (20120713) is fixed -- now we have a vector called dli with distance triplet
	preceeded by its frequency: [freq, dups, loss, ils] where all values are smaller than one. This vector is stored in
	one dimension (using dli[4*i + j] notation)
	* TODO: program is ready to work with ILS; we should simplify structures to work with dli[] vector instead of dups[]
	and loss[] --> this way we can store other values as well, like an extended RF etc. 
	* TODO: for small data sets MC integration might be fair -- e.g. for 5 taxa sptree and 10 leaves gene tree, we have
	around 31 distinct triplet configurations that can be adequately approximated by empirical histograms. But for large
	trees (e.g. 100s of leaves) the number of terms (and pre-calculations) may become huge. In this case our hope is that
	the MCMC approx to the exchange algorithm (e.g. from http://arxiv.org/abs/1007.5192) behaves faster. When updating a
	species tree or the lambda vector, sample the gene trees (maybe in parallel) through a second, auxiliary MCMC chain.
	The parallelization will be handy when calculating lnl since it will decrease the granularity of comm between nodes.
