2012.06.16 Leo Martins
	* bug: found a stupid bug in the normalization constant calculation: it should be {log(freq_i) - dist_i/lambda} and
	not {-log(freq_i)*(dist_i/lambda)}. Ironically, because if this the program was wrongly capable of correctly finding
	the sptree if we only have one gene tree: the problem of using IS trees to calc the normalization is that if there's
	only one IS tree then the normalization does not work. Or if the IS trees are not a good sample in general. The
	distrib of distances should be a good approximation even if the importance space is reduced, since even in this
	reduced space the distances should mean something -- e.g. we only know that the gene trees are close to the sptree b/c
	there are other gene trees that are far apart. 
	* empfreq: now the empfreq structure has an "empfreq_double" version, with same info as "regular" elements (vector i[]) but
	scaled to one *and* taken the log of. That is, if the sum of all freqs (total number of original elements before
	empfreq) is sum_freq, then each element logfreq[i] is log( freq[i]/sum_freq). This empfreq_double is actually just a
	pair of vectors, cannot be manipulated (we don't have empfreq_elements that can be smartly sorted, keeping idx), but
	economize space. It's a "read-only" solution -- but we do change the idx[] vector when we scale dups and losses to be
	between zero and one.
	* min/max distances: up to now the hyperprior on #losses is #leaves X #dups, but it might be better to use empirically 
	determined max/min numbers (not my favorite) or better yet, standardize all values to be smaller than one so that lambdas 
	have same magnitude. (the non-scaled values should be kept in algos and reported to user though). I am trying with min
	equal to zero (forced by hand) or equal to minimum found in MC integration of normalization factor. The problem is
	when the sampled min is not the true min -- that can be found later in the simulation -- b/c we will have a
	standardized value lower than zero (standardization -> x-min/(max-min) )
2012.06.15 Leo Martins
	* IS already works: but it would be best if we separate the IS trees from the normalization trees (that should be an
	expansion of the former). "Best" means more general, but the justification for letting only IS trees is sound: we want
	to work in a space with only the "likely" trees (that is, the contribution of each one *within* this restricted
	space).
	* simulation under ILS: even if I give only one tree for each gene family, and it has zero ILSs, the IS will find the
	species tree but get lost at the rooting (it will find all rootings equaly likely, since our duploss model won't give
	zero dups and losses). Then I realized that this problem was already present in the previous version (presented at
	DMP2011), and I solved it by not applying branch swappings that would change the rooting -- that is, I assumed that we
	knew the root position. When I gave more than one importance tree per gene family, then the results were worse --
	partly because the post distrib of gene trees will become flatter than the importance weights used as input (the
	opposite of BUCKy where the post ditrib becomes more concentrated), since other trees (and lax rooting?) will
	influence it.

2012.06.14 Leo Martins
	* importance sampling: tree file with all possible species trees must be given -- it is the last one on the list of
	tree file names. This allows to precalculate the distribution of #dups and #losses for each sptree (this distrib will
	be specific to each gene family). Therefore this version is (temporarily?) unable to do branch swaps on species trees.
	We can think about ways of insuring that all reasonable sptrees are represented (e.g. using other progs to estimate
	good values and randomize them). It is tempting to simulate gene trees from the prior (to obtain the reasonable
	space), but only if we knew the true species tree! 
	* tree files: the tree files may be better used in the future: one collection of the IS (where frequency of each tree
	matters), and another for the normalization constant calculation (frequencies are irrelevant, but must be a good
	sample of possible values). Right now the same files are used for both. For each sptree and gene family, we have an
	empfreq (structure with "index" and "frequency", emulating a histogram) so that we don't need to store _all_ gene
	trees distances -- although this would be useful to speed up reconciliation calcs a lot, where not for the memory
	problem... Furthermore the tree files can be further compacted (in topology_space) if we keep only distinct *unrooted*
	gene trees (nowadays we compare only the rooted versions, s.t. in the end we might have the same (unrooted) tree
	represented twice b/c of rooting -> not a problem, just a waste). 
	* bug: in previous versions (including the one shown at DMP2011) the importance sampling was chosing the wrong tree:
	instead of copying the treespace->distinct[j] (from the discretely sampled j), is was copying treespace->tree[j] --
	that is, using the longer vector. The IDs by chance were the same, but this could be a problem when there are a lot of
	duplicates (e.g. a non-flat IS of trees). I run one analysis and didn't notice anything weird -- our program, even w/
	the bug, worked well for "sharp" gene tree distributions (where the bug fix would be felt), and worked bad for flat
	distribs with almost all gene trees distinct (in which case the bug didn't affect results). The "guenomu" directory is
	fixed, as well as the "guenomu-IS".
	* TODO: gene_species can be simplified if we only use IS. Exception is to sample from prior (simulate gene trees),
	where we do not use IS info (just to compute empfreq) and apply branch swapping. (In hindsinght, this is our only
	mechanism for diversity generation that does not depend from other programs...)
	* TODO: even if we are not using IS, we can approximate the empfreq of distances for each species tree and gene family
	provided, as before, that all possible sptrees are given in advance. This can be done by simulating gene trees online,
	beforehand. (the drawback is the construction of of the emnpfreq itself, that need a complete (not online) vector of
	dists.)
2011.10.21 Leo Martins
	* WARNING: as you can see this file is not being properly updated since now we are using mercurial (and before that,
	svn) to note down. Just to recapitulate, the program works with importance sampling, and can even simulate from the
	prior. But (as shown in DMP2011 in Munich) we must take care of the normalization constant -- which is dependent on
	the species tree, and must be summed up over all gene trees. Now I'm creating a quasi-random mechanism to calculate
	the sum using Monte Carlo (I think they call this quasi-Monte Carlo). The approach is to sample trees that even if not
	random can spread over all cases.
	* other changes (that I can remember): the program bmc2_tree is tailored for this problem with the normalization
	constant. For "small" datasets (let's say, up to 8 taxa on the gene tree) it can: (given one parameter) simulate many
	trees and compare how many times it samples from the "expected" ones (given be the gene tree file); (given two params)
	calculated all dups and losses between all possible combinations of trees; (given three params) calc all possibilities
	like before -- so the files should have the whole set of trees -- and then simulate to see how the histograms of dups
	and losses approach the expected ones (based on the mean squared error between histograms). 
2010.07.21 Leo
  * summary of old changes: I stopped using this changelog always, now I make more notes on snv. The current version
    calculates the minimum number (over genetree) of dups between gene and species tree; that is, it "finds" the optimal
    root point on gene tree; therefore maybe the gene tree can be unrooted (in the structs as well), but we might want
    to change it later. The SPR also is root-aware, and the user can opt for rerooting and topol change (works well
    combined with prior simulation);
  * mcmc_chain_proposal: I found a mistake in MTM calculations: the weight function has a proposal term related to
    *previous* param value. That is, w(y) = pi(y) X P(x/y) where x is current and y is proposed value. And since 
    P(x/y)=1/(zeta * x) for continous value perturbation (notice that it depends on old x, not new y) then the
    model.lnProb_XXX terms might be useless. We need to recalculate them, depending on "previous" value (furthermore we
    cannot calculate it before the first iteration...)

