2009.09.07 Leonardo de Oliveira Martins (leomrtns at gmail dot com)
  * deep coal TODO (cont.): The paper described below (MPE.1997.349) is incomplete in that: it doesn't describe that the
    "extra nodes" on sptree for duplicates should follow the ordering of the leaves in genetree; it does not explain how
    to calculate the weights of the coalescences (the weigthed version is the number of deep coals, otherwise is wrong);
    My answer: for each unmapped sptree node, do an _inverse_mapping_ - the lca mapping between this node (provided
    we know the genes it refers to) and the genetree - and count the path between the unmapped sptree node and the
    sptree mapping of the inverse-mapped genetree node. The easy way, without directy doing the reverse mapping:
    - when mapping genetree node --> sptree node, associate a bipartition (of gene size) to this sptree node with the
      genetree->node->bp (leaves); if several genetree nodes map to same sptree node, accumulate (bipartition_OR()) the
      leaves on sptree node.
    - after finishing the mapping, postorder-search the sptree nodes which weren't mapped (=empty bipartitions) and, for
      each, climb up the tree until we find a mapped node (in sptree, naturally) containing all genetree leaves spanned
      by the unmapped sptree node (e.g. for an unmapped node (A,B) we search for the sptree node with bipartition
      containing  A1,A2,..,An,B1,...,Bm). The path size is the number of deep coals for this node. 
    - Problem: duplicates => the extra nodes in sptree should follow the original branching pattern in genetree (or
      not?); missing species can be treated "easily" as described yesterday.
    Alternative implementation (following ThanNakhleh.PLoSCP09, page 9):
    - For each sptree->node we have that the number of species is given by sptree-node->bp->n_ones, which would be the
      number of lineages leaving (to the past) sptree->node; but we have coalescences going on below (to the present)
      or _on_ this node (through the mapping; remember that mapping to a node means the coalescence happened before (up
      to the root) but before the parent node).
    - thus the number of extra lineages leaving (from to present to past; for us a node refers to branch above it) 
      sptree->node is equal to "sptree->node->bp->n_ones - #coal(node) - 1"
      where #coal is accumulated in postorder, and includes genetree nodes mapping to sptree->node. (maybe in practice
      is enough to calc this only for unmapped sptree nodes).
2009.09.06 Leonardo de Oliveira Martins (leomrtns at gmail dot com)
  * topology_mrca.*: TODO
  - to calc deep coalescence, see MolPhylogEvol.1997.349 (Slowinski, Knight and Rooney). The number of deepcoal is 
    number of nodes in "embedded" species tree which are not mapped by any node in gene tree. The embedding means that
    species absent in gene tree are removed and artificial nodes in sptree are created for dup species. They don't 
    mention but I assume that these artificial nodes should follow as closely as possible the equiv. nodes in the gene
    tree (e.g. ((A1,A2),(A3,A4)) in gene tree should span ((A,A),(A,A)) on species tree so that all internal nodes are
    mapped). In practice - NEGLECTING the possible absence of species in gene - these duplicate genes mean creating a 
    vector with counts of the representativity of each species tree node: internal nodes have a count of one and if
    mapped by the gene tree are decreased to zero; leaves have a count of the number of duplicates in gene tree (in
    example above leaf "A" of sptree should have a count of 4) and then each time a gene tree node searches for a leaf
    lca mapping the counting decreases by one (e.g. internal node (A1,A2) above will decrease the count of leaf "A").
    The number of deepcoal will be given by the sum of the elements in this vector.
    If we have a species without any representative in gene tree, it becomes more complicated since we must prune the
    species subtrees with these species (the corresponding vector elements should not be counted). Solution: exclusion
    flag in preorder: leaves have flag 0 (present) or 2 (absent); internal nodes have flag 0 (present), 1 (absent but
    don't remove parent node since one child is present), 2 (remove since both children are excluded; parent is eligible
    for removal). In hindsight, this flag is the number of flag-2  children (maybe?).
  - The above solution can also be used as a general algorithm to remove subtrees (like the recomb detection algorithm
    in biomc2 does, using the bipartitions);

