2009.08.19 Leonardo de Oliveira Martins (leomrtns at gmail dot com)
  * random idea: a topology (using our structure) can be compactly stored within just one vector of integers, the parent
  IDs. To make them unique we can use the mid[0] (postorder index) plus nleaves. By uniqueness I mean two instances
  representing same topology sould have same vector. The vector is of size nnodes. This can be used to store in disk a
  lot of trees or keep track of best trees. Since a tree with 1024 leaves have less than 2048 nodes (numbered from 0 to
  2045 actually) each element of the vector can be stored in a "short int" (thus a uint_64 can store 4 elements!). It is
  also a good way of MPI-sending a topology...
  * random idea: when working with codons, create a bidim matrix of variable size (just like char_vector) where for 
    each triplet 0 <= tri < 64 we have a vector of possible neighbours. Ex: the nonzero elements of Q matrix from AAA 
    are only .AA, A.A and AA (only 12 elems instead of 64). This bidim matrix would be used in likelihood (s1 and s2 
    variables, according to current implementation). Another options is to calculate "on the fly" these eligible 
    elements using the binary notation of bases (and codons), maybe using a mask - I don't know the details yet.

2009.08.19 Leonardo de Oliveira Martins (leomrtns at gmail dot com)
  * likelihood.*: So far we have only the simplest function, based only on "down" nodes (left and right), using 
    "current" ring elements, for all "postorder" nodes. In the real MCMC we must: 1) use "up" nodes (up and sister) when
    accepting  a move; 2) distinguish between "accepted" and "current" ring elements, and 3) use only "undone" nodes
    when  necessary. Notice that the functions we use here (ln_likelihood() ) are not used in original biomc2: they use
    only  _moved_branches() that distinguish between "accepted" and "current" and works on a vector of phylogenies
    - topology  moves - and _proposal() which is used when updating model parameters (thus proposal->accepted without
    "current").
  * likelihood.c: must check for underflows, possibly using biomcmc_logspace_add() functions. Also decide if calcs should 
    be done in log scale. UPDATE 2009.08.20: the marginal likelihood over discretized rates uses logspace_add() but only
    one rate contributes to likelihood (the difference in logLiks is of the order 100, so logLik[cat 1] + logLik[cat 2]
    = logLik[cat 1] ). Ziheng (JMolEvol.2000.423) describes Hiro's recurrent hint which is the base for logspace_add(),
    in the context of node scaling (pp 426). Basically at every 50th depth node we reescale all partial likelihoods (for
    a given rate category). IOW, for this elected node we divide all site/pattern likelihoods (which I assume are not 
    in log scale) by the maximum value (over sites/patterns) and then store this maximum value, which will be added (in
    log scale) to the final likelihood for each site. 

2009.08.19 Leonardo de Oliveira Martins (leomrtns at gmail dot com)
  * phylogeny.*: How the likelihood matrix is organized:
    phy->l[node]->d[cycle]->lk[cat][pat][state]   (where "d" is for "down" and we also have "u" for "up"
    * node: 0 <= node < ntax are the leaves (one cycle) and ntax <= node < nnodes are internal nodes (many cycles)
    * cycle: the "node_likelihood" struct is a ring of likelihood vectors (for rejected/accepted proposals)
    * cat: number of categories in discrete gamma rates (evolution_model knows this values as "nrates")
    * pat: number of site patterns (distinct columns)
    * state: 4 for DNA, 64 (or 63)  for codons, known only to evolution_model but now forced at 4

2009.08.18 Leonardo de Oliveira Martins (leomrtns at gmail dot com)
  * I didn't document for the last month, so just broad comments:
  * lowlevel.*: char_vector_struct, which holds sequence names (for alignments and trees) and the
    sequences themselves; we can check (and reduce the matrix sizes) for duplicate or empty columns: this is useful when
    checking if species names are duplicated; we can also read directly from a file into char_vector (to read species
    names.
  * alignment.*: reads FASTA and nexus formats, and can determine the file format. char_vector_struct charshort that gets
    the first nonspaced string of sequence name (common in fasta format) to create topology leaf names;
  * alignment.*: distance_matrix_struct used in calculating the pairwise distances using JC and K2P corrections; also
    calculates average distance, average kappa and empirical equilibrium frequencies. Can also create a smaller
    distance_matrix using only selected elements from a larger distance_matrix;
  * empirical_frequency.*: general ordering of integer vectors by value, preserving index. As the name implies, can be
    used to calculate (by ordering) the empirical distribution of integer values.
  * prob_distribution*: probability distribution functions from R version 2.9.1, and extra math functions (like exp()
    and log() for small numbers. The distributions are the gamma, normal, lognormal and poisson (pgamma, qgamma, dgamma,
    rgamma for instance).
  * random_number.*: included a normal random number generator using Marsaglia's polar method (a rejection method where
    ~27% of the values are rejected), from a Standard Normal (the general case is in prob_distribution.c). At each
    sample two values are generated so we store one for next call. The same reasoning applies when we want just 32bits
    or even 16bits of randomness. The double precision float functions (uniform = unif, standard normal = snorm) also
    have the high- and low-precision versions (using 64 or 32 random bits).
  * topology_build.*: creates a random tree or a upgma tree based on alignment; these functions have the low level
    equivalents that use an initialized topology or a distance_matrix, respectively. We also have a function that
    updates a species tree distance matrix based on the minimum (and maximum as a proof-of-concept) distances from the
    gene family distance matrices. Similar to the Maximum Tree of Liu and Pearl, but with the resulting species tree
    distance matrix producing an UPGMA tree (NJ tree is more complex to implement since we must update the whole tree
    and find the new mindist at every iteration giving O(N^3)). 
  * topology_mrca.*: can already calculate the minimum cost reconciliation between gene and species trees: we can map
    gene names to species names where longer species names are searched before shorter names within genes (such that we
    first look for "EColiII" and only then look for "EColi" on remaining gene names); now topology_struct have a
    "reconciliation" member struct that is initialized by topology_common.c but used internally by topology_mrca.c;
    toplogy_struct also has a vector of size two mid[2] (from map id), which gives the index in postorder[] and undone[]
    node vectors. These vectors make it possible to scan or all internal or just changed nodes, respectively, without a
    reentrant functions - just with a loop - and the mid[] indexes give the bijection (from node->vector).

2009.07.20 Leonardo de Oliveira Martins (leomrtns at gmail dot com)
  * random_number.*: The GFSR4 initial vector now is further randomized by a fast gamerand generator over each 
    element (Ian C. Bullard, http://ianbullard.squarespace.com/journal/2009/4/28/why-you-should-never-use-rand.html), 
    a very simple left and right shifts (I included 32 bits shifts for working with 64bit arch but I don't know if it's 
    necessary). The Tausworthe generator now is combined with an XOR-shift generator (xorgens by R. P. Brent) to avoid
    correlation of streams. A few dieharder tests (the fastest) gave good results, and I think the speed overhead is
    negligible.

2009.07.19 Leonardo de Oliveira Martins (leomrtns at gmail dot com)
  * random_number.*: The seed for the streams is chosen based on a GFSR4 generator, and we have 150 independent
    Tausworthe streams of 64 bits even if initialized with the same seed (streams described in
    http://www.iro.umontreal.ca/~lecuyer/myftp/papers/tausme2.ps). The Tausworthe streams work with a global variable
    "biomcmc_random_number" which is a pointer, so the parallel program can cycle between streams (by just
    "biomcmc_random_number = physically_alloced_rng" where necessary). In practice, the root process should broadcast
    the initial seed and then streams initialized with same seed and stream_number are guaranteed to be the same. 
    Different seeds and same stream_number (modulus 150) lead to different points on same stream (which works for small 
    simulations provided points are distant on the stream). Remember that the seed we provided will be given to an
    independent GFSR generator where first "stream_number" iterations are thrown away.
  * random_number_local: tables with parameter sets for 150 streams, and locally-shared GFSR4 instance. The first 
    streams are better (longer periods). 

2009.07.10 Leonardo de Oliveira Martins (leomrtns at gmail dot com)
  * topology_mrca.*: Since the reconciliation algorithm is based on the LCA between any two nodes of the species tree
    now the rmca functions can handle internal nodes as well. The variable topol_node::level is used to climb up the
    tree always from the node closer to the root.
  * bipartition.c: Following K&R now there is a faster function to calculate the number of bits set in the bipartition.
    It's based on the fact that (u-1) is a swap on all bits lower than the least significant (inclusive). Ex: u = 1100
    then u-1 = 1011. Then u & (u-1) zeroes the last bit set, and the nuber of times we can do this is the number of
    active bits.

