2009.07.05 Leonardo de Oliveira Martins (leomrtns at gmail dot com)
  * lib/topology_space.*: replace nexus_tree.* since now we create a vector of topologies straight from the nexus tree
    file. If the trees (the first tree, actually) don't have branch lengths we store only the distinct topologies,
    otherwise the two vectors (distinct and tree) are the same.
    If a hashtable is given as parameter to topology_space creation function all nexus_trees nodes are renumbered so that
    the topologies' leaves follow order of this external hashtable (e.g. if the hashtable comes from the alignment file
    then the topol leaves have same indexes). The topology_space hashtable then points to this external hashtable and
    taxlabels are reordered accordingly.
    The bipartitions of the leaves are shared among all members of the same topology_space.
  * bipartition.h, hashtable.h: now these structures have a integer counter called ref_counter that describes how many
    times this instance is being used (referenced) in other places. Since (as described before) they are shared by
    several structs, they can be deleted (free() ) only after nobody is referencing them anymore to avoid "double free"
    memory corruption.
  * topology_mrca.*: finds the LCA between a pair of leaves and stores it into a lower triangular matrix to avoid
    redundant calculations. The functions ending in "_for_topology" check against distinct topology instance being used
    by different calls, and functions without it assume the topology is the same (even if is not updated).
    Timing test: find all LCA pairs for 1024 trees of 512 leaves each took 100 seconds, and occupied around 200Mb of
    memory (one topology_mrca:: plus the whole topology_space::)
  * general comment: separate headers into global and local (with local defs from C files going to these local headers)

2009.07.04 Leonardo de Oliveira Martins (leomrtns at gmail dot com)  
  * lib/topology_common.c: function update_subtree_traversal() is the only recursive function, that updates vectors of
    nodes in preorder and postorder; as long as topology doesn't change these vectors can be used; (there are also
    recursive functions like undo_udone() that I haven't checked).
    * relation between postorder and preorder: postorder left-right is equivalent (in inverse order) to a preorder
    right-left; so we can store nodes in only postorder and then if we want a preorder we look at vector from last to
    first element (but if postorder is heavy-child first the this preorder would be light-child first).

